<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Chronicleaf.AI.OpenAI</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Chronicleaf.AI.OpenAI</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The contract, and nothing else in the repository. LLM-OPENAI-ADAPTER-001/002 own one thing: how
      OpenAI's Responses API is spoken. Everything on the Chronicleaf side of a call - the consent, the
      bounds, the evidence the owner authorized, the credential's revision, the operation, the audit
      row - belongs to the packages that already shipped it, so this project cannot reach any of it.

      That is why the credential arrives through OpenAIProviderSettings/IOpenAIApiKeySource rather than
      through Chronicleaf.Application.Secrets.ISecretVault: a project reference to the vault would have
      been the one way for a transport to start deciding things that are not its business, including
      which scope a credential belongs to. The vault-backed implementation of the seam lives in the
      composition root instead, and it is the only place that knows both sides.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../../Chronicleaf.AI/Chronicleaf.AI.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The two abstractions a transport needs, and nothing that would make it a product feature:
      dependency injection to be registered by a composition root, and logging so a refusal can say
      which shape of provider answer it saw. There is no HTTP client package here on purpose - the
      adapter takes an HttpMessageHandler and owns one HttpClient, so the call path is the BCL's and a
      test can substitute the far end of the wire without substituting the wire itself.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Binder" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    What this project deliberately cannot reach is the point of it. There is no reference to the
    credential vault, so an adapter that wanted a plaintext key would have to invent a way to get one.
    There is no reference to the generation preview store, so it cannot re-read the evidence the owner
    authorized, and no reference to the domain records, so it cannot see what a milestone is. What it
    can do is serialise one request, send it to one fixed origin, and translate the answer.
  -->

</Project>
